1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: But the method itself is sound: measure the angle and the distance accurately, and it gives Earth's true size.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: Cleomedes' version also rests on a few simplifications. Let's check them.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: First, parallel rays. That one holds very well, as we've seen.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Second, that Syene sits exactly on the Tropic of Cancer.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: In fact it's a little north of it, by less than a degree. Even there, the noon Sun wasn't quite overhead.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: So no shadow was only nearly true.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: And the tropic drifts, too. In Eratosthenes' time it lay about 0.3 degrees further north than today.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: Third, that Syene lies due south of Alexandria, on the same meridian.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: In fact it's about 3 degrees of longitude further east.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: And the Earth isn't a perfect sphere: it's slightly flattened, by about three tenths of a percent.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERB: Still, the angle came out well. The two cities' latitudes really differ by about 7.1 degrees.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERA: Against his seven point two. That's close.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: What we can't check is the distance, because we don't know his stadion.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: Measuring the Earth was only part of what he did.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: In his Geographica, he mapped the known world on a grid of parallels and meridians.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: It named more than 400 cities with their locations, and it gave us the word geography.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: He also measured the tilt of the Earth's axis, and worked out dates in history back to the fall of Troy.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: And his name lives on in mathematics, in the sieve of Eratosthenes.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: The way of finding prime numbers?

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: Write out the numbers. Cross out the multiples of 2, then of 3, then of 5, then of 7.
